<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Warnings are errors across every project here, and the reason is numeric:
         this port's failure mode is a silent conversion, not a crash. A narrowing
         cast, a sign-extended operand in a bitwise or (CS0675) or a nullable slip in
         a hot path changes a result without failing a build, and the maintenance
         contract in CLAUDE.md cannot survive warnings that scroll past. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The browser (wasm) compile gate, repeated here because this file BLOCKS the one that
    would otherwise supply it.

    MSBuild walks up from a project directory and stops at the first Directory.Build.props
    it finds. This repo is a submodule of MatterCAD (at Submodules/colmap-sharp), so this
    file is that first hit and MatterCAD's root Directory.Build.props never evaluates for
    anything under here. MatterCAD's file expects to cover every project under its root, and
    it is what carries the browser gate; without this block the walk would stop one level
    short of the reconstruction library.

    What that would cost: MatterCAD's browser build runs the library chain with
    -p:BrowserCompatGate=true so that CA1416 becomes an error and any API annotated
    [UnsupportedOSPlatform("browser")] that is reached unguarded fails in CI rather than
    when the wasm head is built. Global -p: properties DO flow into this project (they
    reach the whole graph), but SupportedPlatform is an ItemGroup, and items do not - so
    the property would arrive and the item would not, and the analyzer would have no
    browser platform to check against. This library is meant to run in a browser like
    manifold-sharp does, and IsAotCompatible in ColmapSharp.csproj is the promise it keeps
    there, so it cannot be the one link in the chain nobody polices.

    Deliberately a copy rather than an import of the outer file: this repo builds standalone
    from its own solution too, where there is no outer file to import, and a conditional
    import on a path that only exists in one checkout layout is worse than eight lines.
    Everything is conditioned on the property, so a normal build is unchanged.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(BrowserCompatGate)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CA1416</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(BrowserCompatGate)' == 'true'">
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
